<%#
  Inline "upload a new file and attach it" — two flows, one per deployment (spec §2), reusing the
  library upload machinery. Locals: upload_url (share/s3 multipart write-and-attach), attach_url
  (chunk final JSON attach step), redirect_url.

  OPTIONAL LOCAL `primary:` — whether this uploader is THE action of the page it lands on
  (decision 3 of the UI-cohesion spec). It DEFAULTS TO FALSE, which is the load-bearing half: a
  caller that forgets gets the quiet face rather than another accent button. ONE call site is left,
  projects/_log_entry.html.erb, and it does not pass it — a log row's upload is not what the page
  exists for. The Files page's current-project uploader and Home's hero went first, and the project
  page's Files panel — the one caller that passed true — went when Upload moved into the top bar
  (redesign slice 4; layouts/_topbar_upload.html.erb), which is now the hub's primary.

  THE CONTROL IS A <button> BESIDE ITS HIDDEN FILE INPUT, the top bar's shape (UX audit I): it was a
  <label> wrapping the input, which a keyboard cannot reach. upload_forward_controller.js#choose
  opens the input in the same `.uploader`, so the input keeps its own change handling unchanged.
%>
<% upload_btn_class = local_assigns[:primary] ? "upload-btn primary" : "upload-btn" %>
<% upload_btn_data = { controller: "upload-forward", action: "upload-forward#choose" } %>
<% if capabilities.port_writable? %>
  <%# THE SHARE/S3 FORM IS SUBMITTED BY zip_upload_controller.js, mounted with NO unpack door, so it
      only submits: the server attaches what it receives and must get a zip as it is. The controller
      writes "Uploading NAME…" into the status line beside the button while the browser POSTs, which
      the inline `onchange="this.form.requestSubmit()"` it replaced never said (UX audit B8). %>
  <%= form_with url: upload_url, method: :post, class: "uploader",
                data: { turbo: false, controller: "zip-upload" }, html: { multipart: true } do |f| %>
    <%# Where to send the browser after the upload+attach. OPTIONAL: omitted, the controller
        defaults to the project page, which is also what the one caller — a build-log entry —
        passes. This mirrors the chunk branch's
        data-uploader-redirect-url-value below — the two flows now land in the same
        place. Projects::UploadsController re-validates it with url_from (a relative path only), so
        this field can never become an open redirect. %>
    <% if local_assigns[:redirect_url].present? %>
      <%= hidden_field_tag :redirect_url, redirect_url %>
    <% end %>
    <%# `include_hidden: false` IS LOAD-BEARING. `f.file_field` with `multiple: true` renders a
        companion `<input name="file[]" type="hidden" value="">` so an empty selection still
        submits the key — which put a bare "" at the head of `params[:file]`, and the server
        called `original_filename` on a String and 500'd. Nothing needs that marker here: the
        field's own change action is the only thing that submits this form, and it returns on an
        empty selection. Found by uploading four real files through the real form; the
        Rack::Test suites POSTed a Ruby array and never sent the marker at all, which is §14d's
        "assert the rendered field name" with a different attribute. %>
    <%= f.file_field :file, multiple: true, include_hidden: false, hidden: true,
                     data: { zip_upload_target: "input", action: "change->zip-upload#change" } %>
    <%= tag.button type: "button", class: upload_btn_class, data: upload_btn_data do %>
      <span>Upload &amp; attach</span>
    <% end %>
    <span class="upload-status" data-zip-upload-target="status"></span>
  <% end %>
<% else %>
  <span class="uploader" data-controller="uploader"
        data-uploader-begin-url-value="<%= begin_upload_files_path %>"
        data-uploader-finalize-url-value="<%= finalize_upload_files_path %>"
        data-uploader-attach-url-value="<%= attach_url %>"
        data-uploader-redirect-url-value="<%= redirect_url %>"
        data-uploader-parent-node-id-value="<%= local_assigns[:parent_node_id] %>">
    <input type="file" multiple data-uploader-target="input" data-action="uploader#upload" hidden>
    <%= tag.button type: "button", class: upload_btn_class, data: upload_btn_data do %>
      <span>Upload &amp; attach</span>
    <% end %>
    <span class="upload-status" data-uploader-target="status"></span>
  </span>
<% end %>
